*Instructions*
1. Once you buy your materials take them out the packaging.
2. Get your Pixelblaze out and connect it to your computer or any reliable power source.
3. When connected to a power source it will show up on your wifi list, connect to it and it will bring you to a website so it can
connect to your internet, put in the needed information for it to work.
4. Once connected to the wifi, connect your lights correctly to see if the LEDs work, it's very dependent on which way you connect 
them so I recommend getting the right set of wires for connection unless you want to make it harder for yourself.
5. Print out your mask.
6. Since you most likely can't use one strip of LEDs to cover some of the mask, you'll need to map out where you want to put it on 
your mask, then glue it on.
7. After mapping out where you want it on the mask, you need to map it out on the Pixelblaze, and this part actually requires coding
instead of just pressing buttons, there is a github on how to do.
https://github.com/simap/pixelblaze/blob/master/README.mapper.md
8. Test to make sure your LEDs are working, your mapping may be wrong, or the wires aren't fully connected.
OPTIONAL. If your using a mask and not a helmet you should use a mask strap, glue it onto your mask and make sure it's in an area
that doesn't ruin it.
9. The final step. So that the LEDs aren't completely showing their wiring, cover them in a black film so they're more hidden.
